From 82b9a7dc7b8199ffd7b53821fda46b1cd91e0643 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 21:21:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08379/11362] REQ-ARC-REGISTRY-CACHE-1: mtime-gated,
 lock-protected cache for ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml parses

Profiling a live sp80 gate-timeout regression (bisected separately)
found arc_solve_learning.recommend_approach costing 1.7s/call, almost
entirely yaml.safe_load re-parsing the 452KB/6883-line registry from
scratch on every call. Two call sites (arc_solve_learning._registry,
arc_primitive_library._load_registry) both re-read unconditionally.

An unconditional cache was tried first and broke a real test: the
conductor runs concurrently with the test suite and genuinely appends
to this file mid-session, so a cache with no invalidation served a
stale snapshot for the rest of the process. Root-caused by reverting
entirely and confirming the same assertion fails identically on
unmodified code -- that specific test's threshold was already stale
against the live registry, independent of caching, which is what made
the diagnosis take two passes.

Fixed with an mtime-gated cache instead: a cheap stat() serves the
fast path when the file hasn't changed (the actual target -- a single
short-lived game-eval subprocess, where the file cannot change
mid-run), while a genuinely-changed mtime forces a correct re-read
(the test suite, where the conductor can mutate it live).

Adversarial review caught two more real gaps before this landed:
(1) no lock around the check-then-write, while a real competition
submission runs every game's E3AgentPolicy on separate threads in one
process (Swarm.main()) -- N threads' near-simultaneous first calls
could each miss the cache and redundantly reparse, defeating the
fix's own purpose for its highest-value target; (2) arc_solve_learning's
cache was a single un-keyed slot unlike its sibling, a structural (if
then-empirically-safe) test-isolation gap. Both fixed to match the
RunLocalMechanicLedger locking convention already used in this
codebase, with a concurrency test proving exactly one parse occurs
across 8 threads racing a never-before-cached path.

Does NOT fully resolve the sp80/ft09 gate timeout on its own -- the
dominant, budget-scaling costs (object_centric_digest, rich_action_
candidates) are untouched; that's separate, larger follow-up work.
